WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:10.590
"First Man" starring Ryan Gosling follows
Neil Armstrong's journey to become the

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.929
first person to ever walk on the moon.
No spoilers, but we all know how it turns

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:21.750
out. But how did the filmmakers recreate
the moon landing? This is Nathan Crowley,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.880
he's the movie's production designer. He
told us: how he made a trip to space seem

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.439
real, how he actually staged the moon
landing, and how he managed to fit an

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:37.200
entire rocket ship inside a soundstage
in Atlanta. A production designer manages

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.739
and designs just about everything
physical you see in front of a camera

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:48.020
except for costumes. Crowley has a lot of
experience with space and flight

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.410
[Crowley] Everything in your toolbox you've got to
use. [Narrator] Before walking on the moon they

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.270
first had to get there. Gosling rides in
a variety of spacecraft in the movie.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:03.270
Crowley built the lunar module and space
capsule to scale, meanwhile the Saturn 5

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:10.470
model is only 14 feet tall about 125th
the size of the actual rocket - yet it

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:15.869
looks exactly like it. Some of the models
could actually move. The backdrop

00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:20.100
meanwhile showed a rough image of what
would be happening during the scene.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.630
During some scenes, this could help
actors get a better sense of what they

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.009
were acting against in real time. Crowley
and the crew had ample historical

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.689
details to draw upon to bring the story
to life. One of the most complicated sets

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:39.329
to design were the cockpits. [Crowley] Everyone knows all these cockpits so they have to be exact in

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:44.399
the sequential way that the alarms would
go on. [Narrator] An even harder thing to make

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:49.380
convincing; floating in zero gravity. It
involves some ingenuity. Some parts of

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.210
the zero gravity sequences in "First Man"
involved wire work. In other parts they

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.500
put the camera on the ground and film
from below to give the appearance of

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.180
being suspended in the air. This was a
technique Stanley Kubrick pioneered on

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.610
the set of "2001: A Space Odyssey"
which was made long before CGI. Crowley

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.400
is all about finding a perfect balance
between CGI and practical effects. [Crowley] I have

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.440
a fondness for doing things practically
until you can't. For practical design

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.550
to immerse the audience I believe
strongly that you have

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:28.530
to convince them that they're not
looking at fakery so to do that you have

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:33.330
to mix your mediums and make it as
complex as possible and go unnoticed. [Narrator] So

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.010
basically they need to live in perfect
harmony with one another and while much

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.049
attention was paid to how the NASA crew
got to space the movie also had to nail

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:48.180
the actual moon landing. A lot of movies
set in space will use natural scenery

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:54.170
for planets. "The Martian" used the Wadi
Rum desert in Jordan in place of Mars, and

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:59.099
"Interstellar" used Iceland for its ice
planet scenes. [Crowley] We made this film in

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:04.110
Atlanta, Georgia, and anyone who knows
Georgia knows it's pretty green down

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:11.190
there. [Narrator] So not an ideal stand-in for a
grey rocky surface. Some parts were shot

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.959
in a nearby quarry to best capture the
horizon line. Other parts of the moon

00:03:15.959 --> 00:03:19.859
landing were done on a soundstage where
they lit it in just the right way to

00:03:19.859 --> 00:03:24.180
capture the moon's unique shadows. [Crowley] In
design there's no fixing it later, you've got

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.790
to plan it figure it out and then do it
and if during that journey you get

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.880
something more interesting because you
hadn't foreseen something then that

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.200
enhances the film. [Narrator] And in a year when
everyone seems to be thinking about the

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:44.950
moon-landing... [Cardi B] We went to the moon in 1969.
[Narrator] Audiences will be taking an extra close

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:49.390
look at "First Man." [Crowley] The true art of
production design is for the audience

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:58.849
not to notice the design and just to be
in the story.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.510
you

